The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements concerning the situation in the Sudan, in particular resolutions 1591 (2005), 2620 (2022), 2724 (2024), 2736 (2024), 2750 (2024), and 2772 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, unity, independence, and territorial integrity of the Sudan, and emphasizing that the primary responsibility for the protection of civilians lies with the State,

Expressing grave alarm at the continuing escalation of violence, the devastating humanitarian catastrophe, and the spread of famine conditions across the Sudan, which has resulted in the world’s largest displacement crisis,

Condemning in the strongest terms the continued attacks, shelling, and siege operations carried out by the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) against civilians and civilian infrastructure, particularly in El Fasher and surrounding areas in North Darfur, and expressing profound horror at the reported ethnically motivated violence, summary executions in Salha and Al-Malha, and brutal attacks on internally displaced persons and refugees in the Zamzam and Abu Shouk camps,

Condemning also all indiscriminate attacks, including airstrikes and drone strikes causing civilian casualties and damage to essential infrastructure in Khartoum, Atbara, Dongola, and other civilian population centers, and reminding all parties to the conflict of their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law,

Deeply concerned by the announcement of parallel governing structures, in particular the purported “Government of Peace and Unity”, and underscoring that any attempt to establish parallel state institutions or partition the country undermines the sovereignty, unity, and territorial integrity of the Sudan and severely exacerbates the conflict,

Expressing outrage at the detention of United Nations Interim Security Force for Abyei (UNISFA) personnel, the abduction of United Nations and humanitarian workers, and the seizure of vital fuel and humanitarian convoys, and stressing that attacks on peacekeepers and humanitarian personnel may constitute war crimes under international law,

Recalling the Jeddah Declaration of Commitment to Protect the Civilians of Sudan signed on 11 May 2023, and urging the parties to fully implement their commitments without delay,

Reiterating its serious concern over the continued illicit flow of weapons and ammunition into the Sudan, in direct violation of the arms embargo on Darfur established pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005), and condemning any external interference that fuels the conflict,

Emphasizing that there is no viable military solution to the conflict and reaffirming its unwavering support for the Sudanese people’s aspirations for a peaceful, unified, and democratic future under inclusive, civilian-led governance,

1. Demands that all parties to the conflict immediately cease all hostilities and agree to a comprehensive, nationwide ceasefire, and urges the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) to re-engage in good faith in internationally supported peace negotiations;

2. Demands that the RSF immediately halt its siege and all attacks on El Fasher, withdraw its fighters from civilian areas, and immediately end all assaults on internally displaced persons camps, including the Zamzam and Abu Shouk camps;

3. Calls upon all parties to the conflict to immediately cease all unlawful attacks on civilians, civilian infrastructure, and critical facilities, including hospitals, energy facilities, and water networks, and to take all necessary measures to prevent and eliminate conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence;

4. Rejects any attempts to establish parallel governance authorities or fragment the country, affirms that such actions threaten the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Sudan, and calls on all Sudanese stakeholders and international partners to uphold the unity and independence of the Sudanese State;

5. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate the full, safe, rapid, and unhindered access of humanitarian assistance to all people in need across the Sudan, through all available cross-border routes, including the continued and unimpeded operation of the Adré border crossing, and across conflict lines, in accordance with international humanitarian law and the Jeddah Declaration;

6. Strongly condemns the detention and abduction of United Nations and humanitarian personnel, demands the immediate and unconditional release of all detained UNISFA peacekeepers, civilian staff, and abducted humanitarian workers, as well as the immediate return of all seized vehicles and supplies, and insists that all parties guarantee the safety and security of humanitarian and United Nations personnel and assets;

7. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from any external interference that fuels the conflict and instability, and reminds all Member States of their obligations to strictly adhere to the arms embargo measures established in resolution 1591 (2005), as extended by resolution 2772 (2025);

8. Emphasizes the imperative of accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law committed in the Sudan, and calls upon all parties to cooperate fully with international investigative mechanisms and the International Criminal Court, pursuant to resolution 1593 (2005);

9. Reaffirms its commitment to supporting a coordinated, inclusive, and civilian-led political process that restores constitutional order and leads to a democratic transition, reflecting the will of the Sudanese people;

10. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council every 60 days on the implementation of this resolution, the protection of civilians, and the status of humanitarian access throughout the Sudan;

11. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

